1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,817
For almost a hundred years,

2
00:00:01,817 --> 00:00:04,307
one book sat in nearly every American

3
00:00:04,307 --> 00:00:04,643
home.

4
00:00:04,823 --> 00:00:06,010
It wasn't the Bible,

5
00:00:06,010 --> 00:00:08,147
though some people joked that it was

6
00:00:08,147 --> 00:00:09,096
read more often.

7
00:00:09,276 --> 00:00:11,187
It was thicker than a phone book,

8
00:00:11,187 --> 00:00:13,272
and it could get you almost anything

9
00:00:13,272 --> 00:00:14,315
you could imagine.

10
00:00:14,495 --> 00:00:16,620
Clothes, tools, toys,

11
00:00:16,620 --> 00:00:19,453
furniture, a sewing machine,

12
00:00:19,453 --> 00:00:22,691
a shotgun, even an entire house.

13
00:00:22,871 --> 00:00:24,648
It was the Sears catalog.

14
00:00:24,998 --> 00:00:27,024
If you grew up between the nineteen

15
00:00:27,024 --> 00:00:28,994
fifties and the nineteen eighties,

16
00:00:28,994 --> 00:00:29,920
you remember it.

17
00:00:30,100 --> 00:00:32,956
The fat spring and fall catalogs.

18
00:00:33,136 --> 00:00:34,079
And above all,

19
00:00:34,079 --> 00:00:35,697
the Christmas Wish Book,

20
00:00:35,697 --> 00:00:38,190
which arrived every fall and got worn

21
00:00:38,190 --> 00:00:40,548
out by kids circling every toy they

22
00:00:40,548 --> 00:00:41,019
wanted.

23
00:00:41,199 --> 00:00:43,975
And then, in nineteen ninety-three,

24
00:00:43,975 --> 00:00:44,926
it was gone.

25
00:00:45,277 --> 00:00:47,738
Today, let's tell the whole story.

26
00:00:47,918 --> 00:00:51,167
How a railroad station agent in Minnesota

27
00:00:51,167 --> 00:00:54,100
built the biggest store in the world,

28
00:00:54,100 --> 00:00:57,033
how the catalog changed life in rural

29
00:00:57,033 --> 00:00:59,727
America, what it was like to order

30
00:00:59,727 --> 00:01:01,867
from it, and how one of the

31
00:01:01,867 --> 00:01:05,117
most powerful companies in history let it

32
00:01:05,117 --> 00:01:06,227
all slip away.

33
00:01:06,577 --> 00:01:09,807
The story begins in eighteen eighty-six,

34
00:01:09,807 --> 00:01:11,179
in North Redwood,

35
00:01:11,179 --> 00:01:11,987
Minnesota.

36
00:01:12,167 --> 00:01:15,275
A young railroad station agent named Richard

37
00:01:15,275 --> 00:01:18,596
Sears received a shipment of gold-filled pocket

38
00:01:18,596 --> 00:01:21,422
watches that a local jeweler had refused

39
00:01:21,422 --> 00:01:22,128
to accept.

40
00:01:22,308 --> 00:01:24,276
Instead of sending them back,

41
00:01:24,276 --> 00:01:25,905
Sears sold them himself,

42
00:01:25,905 --> 00:01:28,280
to other station agents up and down

43
00:01:28,280 --> 00:01:28,891
the line.

44
00:01:29,071 --> 00:01:30,429
He made a quick profit.

45
00:01:30,780 --> 00:01:33,148
Sears realized he had found something.

46
00:01:33,328 --> 00:01:35,838
He started a mail-order watch business,

47
00:01:35,838 --> 00:01:38,156
and soon he needed someone who could

48
00:01:38,156 --> 00:01:39,121
repair watches.

49
00:01:39,301 --> 00:01:41,051
He put an ad in the paper,

50
00:01:41,051 --> 00:01:44,080
and a watchmaker named Alvah Roebuck answered

51
00:01:44,080 --> 00:01:44,282
it.

52
00:01:44,462 --> 00:01:46,628
By eighteen ninety-three,

53
00:01:46,628 --> 00:01:49,140
the company was called Sears,

54
00:01:49,140 --> 00:01:50,872
Roebuck and Company,

55
00:01:50,872 --> 00:01:52,345
based in Chicago.

56
00:01:52,696 --> 00:01:55,354
Richard Sears was a born salesman.

57
00:01:55,534 --> 00:01:58,081
He wrote most of the early catalog

58
00:01:58,081 --> 00:02:00,852
copy himself, full of bold claims and

59
00:02:00,852 --> 00:02:02,500
exciting descriptions.

60
00:02:02,680 --> 00:02:04,467
By eighteen ninety-four,

61
00:02:04,467 --> 00:02:07,296
the catalog was more than five hundred

62
00:02:07,296 --> 00:02:10,795
pages long, selling everything from bicycles to

63
00:02:10,795 --> 00:02:13,327
sewing machines to baby carriages.

64
00:02:13,507 --> 00:02:15,985
He called it the Cheapest Supply House

65
00:02:15,985 --> 00:02:16,572
on Earth.

66
00:02:16,921 --> 00:02:19,596
To understand why the catalog mattered,

67
00:02:19,596 --> 00:02:22,065
you have to picture rural America in

68
00:02:22,065 --> 00:02:23,574
the eighteen nineties.

69
00:02:23,754 --> 00:02:26,774
Most Americans still lived on farms or

70
00:02:26,774 --> 00:02:27,887
in tiny towns.

71
00:02:28,067 --> 00:02:30,572
The nearest store was a general store

72
00:02:30,572 --> 00:02:33,077
that might carry a few hundred items,

73
00:02:33,077 --> 00:02:34,499
often at high prices.

74
00:02:34,679 --> 00:02:36,607
If you wanted something special,

75
00:02:36,607 --> 00:02:37,872
you were out of luck.

76
00:02:38,221 --> 00:02:40,611
Then, in eighteen ninety-six,

77
00:02:40,611 --> 00:02:44,235
the Post Office started Rural Free Delivery.

78
00:02:44,415 --> 00:02:45,677
For the first time,

79
00:02:45,677 --> 00:02:48,200
mail came right to the farmhouse door.

80
00:02:48,380 --> 00:02:50,339
And in nineteen thirteen,

81
00:02:50,339 --> 00:02:51,749
Parcel Post began,

82
00:02:51,749 --> 00:02:54,568
which meant packages could come too.

83
00:02:54,748 --> 00:02:57,725
Suddenly, a farm family in Kansas could

84
00:02:57,725 --> 00:03:00,091
buy the same things as a family

85
00:03:00,091 --> 00:03:02,457
in Chicago, at the same prices.

86
00:03:02,807 --> 00:03:05,621
The Sears catalog became a window to

87
00:03:05,621 --> 00:03:06,871
the wider world.

88
00:03:07,051 --> 00:03:09,814
Farm families studied it by lamplight.

89
00:03:09,994 --> 00:03:12,119
Children learned to read from it.

90
00:03:12,299 --> 00:03:14,493
Immigrants learned English from it,

91
00:03:14,493 --> 00:03:16,687
matching the pictures to the words.

92
00:03:16,867 --> 00:03:19,420
Many families called it the Wish Book

93
00:03:19,420 --> 00:03:22,387
long before Sears officially gave that name

94
00:03:22,387 --> 00:03:24,112
to the Christmas catalog.

95
00:03:24,461 --> 00:03:26,064
Not everyone was happy.

96
00:03:26,244 --> 00:03:29,313
Small-town shopkeepers hated the catalog,

97
00:03:29,313 --> 00:03:32,083
because it took their customers away.

98
00:03:32,263 --> 00:03:33,381
In some towns,

99
00:03:33,381 --> 00:03:37,051
merchants held bonfires and burned catalogs in

100
00:03:37,051 --> 00:03:37,929
the street.

101
00:03:38,109 --> 00:03:41,074
Some offered prizes to anyone who brought

102
00:03:41,074 --> 00:03:41,581
one in.

103
00:03:41,761 --> 00:03:43,402
To avoid embarrassment,

104
00:03:43,402 --> 00:03:46,400
Sears began shipping orders in plain brown

105
00:03:46,400 --> 00:03:49,754
packages, so neighbors couldn't tell where they

106
00:03:49,754 --> 00:03:50,468
came from.

107
00:03:50,818 --> 00:03:53,488
The catalog also had a second life.

108
00:03:53,668 --> 00:03:55,500
When the new one arrived,

109
00:03:55,500 --> 00:03:57,626
the old one often went out to

110
00:03:57,626 --> 00:04:00,411
the outhouse, where its pages served a

111
00:04:00,411 --> 00:04:02,097
very practical purpose.

112
00:04:02,277 --> 00:04:04,262
People still joke about it today.

113
00:04:04,442 --> 00:04:07,068
Some say the glossy color pages were

114
00:04:07,068 --> 00:04:08,381
the least popular,

115
00:04:08,381 --> 00:04:09,841
for obvious reasons.

116
00:04:10,191 --> 00:04:11,923
In eighteen ninety-five,

117
00:04:11,923 --> 00:04:15,749
a Chicago clothing businessman named Julius Rosenwald

118
00:04:15,749 --> 00:04:17,482
bought into the company.

119
00:04:17,662 --> 00:04:20,576
Rosenwald was a brilliant organizer.

120
00:04:20,756 --> 00:04:23,146
Orders were pouring in by the thousands

121
00:04:23,146 --> 00:04:25,536
every day, and the company could barely

122
00:04:25,536 --> 00:04:26,027
keep up.

123
00:04:26,207 --> 00:04:27,925
He set out to build a better

124
00:04:27,925 --> 00:04:28,354
system.

125
00:04:28,705 --> 00:04:30,338
In nineteen oh six,

126
00:04:30,338 --> 00:04:33,347
Sears opened a giant new complex on

127
00:04:33,347 --> 00:04:35,067
Chicago's West Side.

128
00:04:35,247 --> 00:04:37,539
It was one of the largest buildings

129
00:04:37,539 --> 00:04:38,390
in the world,

130
00:04:38,390 --> 00:04:39,700
with conveyor belts,

131
00:04:39,700 --> 00:04:42,516
chutes, and a timetable system that tracked

132
00:04:42,516 --> 00:04:44,546
every order down to the minute.

133
00:04:44,726 --> 00:04:47,081
Henry Ford is said to have visited

134
00:04:47,081 --> 00:04:49,504
to study it before building his own

135
00:04:49,504 --> 00:04:50,473
assembly line.

136
00:04:50,824 --> 00:04:54,457
Rosenwald also introduced a famous promise.

137
00:04:54,637 --> 00:04:58,039
Satisfaction guaranteed, or your money back.

138
00:04:58,219 --> 00:05:00,353
For farm families who had never seen

139
00:05:00,353 --> 00:05:02,428
the company and could not touch the

140
00:05:02,428 --> 00:05:04,384
products before they bought them,

141
00:05:04,384 --> 00:05:06,044
that promise was everything.

142
00:05:06,224 --> 00:05:08,128
It built a kind of trust that

143
00:05:08,128 --> 00:05:09,638
lasted for generations.

144
00:05:09,988 --> 00:05:12,642
Rosenwald became one of the richest men

145
00:05:12,642 --> 00:05:14,752
in America, and he gave much of

146
00:05:14,752 --> 00:05:15,909
his fortune away.

147
00:05:16,089 --> 00:05:17,563
Working with Booker T.

148
00:05:17,743 --> 00:05:20,781
Washington, he helped fund the construction of

149
00:05:20,781 --> 00:05:23,884
nearly five thousand schools for Black children

150
00:05:23,884 --> 00:05:25,139
in the rural South.

151
00:05:25,319 --> 00:05:27,654
They were known as Rosenwald Schools,

152
00:05:27,654 --> 00:05:29,863
and some of them are still standing

153
00:05:29,863 --> 00:05:30,241
today.

154
00:05:30,592 --> 00:05:33,669
Perhaps the most amazing thing Sears ever

155
00:05:33,669 --> 00:05:34,945
sold was a house.

156
00:05:35,125 --> 00:05:38,326
From nineteen oh eight to nineteen forty,

157
00:05:38,326 --> 00:05:41,370
Sears sold kit homes through its Modern

158
00:05:41,370 --> 00:05:42,463
Homes catalog.

159
00:05:42,643 --> 00:05:44,063
You picked a design,

160
00:05:44,063 --> 00:05:45,412
sent in your order,

161
00:05:45,412 --> 00:05:46,973
and a few weeks later,

162
00:05:46,973 --> 00:05:50,168
a railroad boxcar arrived with everything you

163
00:05:50,168 --> 00:05:50,665
needed.

164
00:05:51,016 --> 00:05:53,558
That included about thirty thousand pieces.

165
00:05:53,738 --> 00:05:57,570
Precut lumber, numbered so you'd know where

166
00:05:57,570 --> 00:05:58,996
each piece went,

167
00:05:58,996 --> 00:06:00,868
plus nails, shingles,

168
00:06:00,868 --> 00:06:04,878
windows, doors, paint and a seventy-five page

169
00:06:04,878 --> 00:06:06,393
instruction book.

170
00:06:06,573 --> 00:06:09,190
Prices ranged from a few hundred dollars

171
00:06:09,190 --> 00:06:11,023
for a small cottage to a few

172
00:06:11,023 --> 00:06:13,051
thousand for a big family home.

173
00:06:13,401 --> 00:06:16,215
Sears sold roughly seventy thousand of these

174
00:06:16,215 --> 00:06:17,813
homes across the country.

175
00:06:17,993 --> 00:06:20,242
Many families built them with the help

176
00:06:20,242 --> 00:06:21,367
of their neighbors,

177
00:06:21,367 --> 00:06:23,438
like an old-fashioned barn raising.

178
00:06:23,618 --> 00:06:26,006
And many of those houses are still

179
00:06:26,006 --> 00:06:27,972
standing and lived in today.

180
00:06:28,152 --> 00:06:30,830
Some owners don't even know their house

181
00:06:30,830 --> 00:06:32,204
came from a catalog.

182
00:06:32,554 --> 00:06:34,335
In the nineteen twenties,

183
00:06:34,335 --> 00:06:36,187
America started to change.

184
00:06:36,367 --> 00:06:38,773
Cars and roads made it easy for

185
00:06:38,773 --> 00:06:40,791
people to drive into town.

186
00:06:40,971 --> 00:06:43,195
So in nineteen twenty-five,

187
00:06:43,195 --> 00:06:46,573
under a leader named General Robert Wood,

188
00:06:46,573 --> 00:06:49,538
Sears opened its first retail store,

189
00:06:49,538 --> 00:06:50,445
in Chicago.

190
00:06:50,625 --> 00:06:51,634
Within a few years,

191
00:06:51,634 --> 00:06:53,121
there were hundreds of them.

192
00:06:53,471 --> 00:06:56,187
By the nineteen fifties and sixties,

193
00:06:56,187 --> 00:06:58,602
the Sears store was a fixture of

194
00:06:58,602 --> 00:06:59,659
American life.

195
00:06:59,839 --> 00:07:02,534
It anchored the new shopping centers and

196
00:07:02,534 --> 00:07:02,939
malls.

197
00:07:03,119 --> 00:07:06,176
Families went there for Kenmore washers,

198
00:07:06,176 --> 00:07:10,456
Craftsman tools, DieHard batteries and Toughskins jeans.

199
00:07:10,636 --> 00:07:12,795
If you bought a refrigerator in the

200
00:07:12,795 --> 00:07:14,955
sixties, there was a good chance it

201
00:07:14,955 --> 00:07:15,942
came from Sears.

202
00:07:16,292 --> 00:07:18,539
A trip to Sears was a family

203
00:07:18,539 --> 00:07:19,101
outing.

204
00:07:19,281 --> 00:07:21,940
Dad headed straight for the tool department.

205
00:07:22,120 --> 00:07:25,022
Mom looked at appliances and towels.

206
00:07:25,202 --> 00:07:27,855
The kids pressed their noses against the

207
00:07:27,855 --> 00:07:29,844
toy counter or the candy case,

208
00:07:29,844 --> 00:07:32,630
where warm cashews and chocolate were sold

209
00:07:32,630 --> 00:07:33,492
by the pound.

210
00:07:33,672 --> 00:07:35,984
And everybody met up again at the

211
00:07:35,984 --> 00:07:37,666
snack bar for a hot dog.

212
00:07:38,016 --> 00:07:40,718
And many Sears stores had a catalog

213
00:07:40,718 --> 00:07:41,104
desk.

214
00:07:41,284 --> 00:07:43,599
You could flip through the catalog in

215
00:07:43,599 --> 00:07:45,352
the store, place your order,

216
00:07:45,352 --> 00:07:47,041
and come back to pick it up

217
00:07:47,041 --> 00:07:47,855
a week later.

218
00:07:48,035 --> 00:07:49,288
In the seventies,

219
00:07:49,288 --> 00:07:52,016
Sears was the largest retailer in the

220
00:07:52,016 --> 00:07:52,458
world.

221
00:07:52,638 --> 00:07:53,888
By some estimates,

222
00:07:53,888 --> 00:07:56,525
about two out of every three Americans

223
00:07:56,525 --> 00:07:58,815
shopped at Sears in a given year.

224
00:07:59,164 --> 00:08:00,267
For kids, though,

225
00:08:00,267 --> 00:08:02,732
the catalog that mattered most was the

226
00:08:02,732 --> 00:08:04,029
Christmas Wish Book.

227
00:08:04,209 --> 00:08:07,531
Sears published its first Christmas catalog in

228
00:08:07,531 --> 00:08:10,780
nineteen thirty-three, during the Depression.

229
00:08:10,960 --> 00:08:12,688
By the fifties and sixties,

230
00:08:12,688 --> 00:08:14,864
it arrived in the mail every fall,

231
00:08:14,864 --> 00:08:17,040
and it was the most exciting thing

232
00:08:17,040 --> 00:08:19,087
that came to the house all year.

233
00:08:19,438 --> 00:08:21,503
Children spent hours with it,

234
00:08:21,503 --> 00:08:23,710
lying on the living room floor.

235
00:08:23,890 --> 00:08:26,219
Cowboy outfits, dolls,

236
00:08:26,219 --> 00:08:29,078
train sets, chemistry sets,

237
00:08:29,078 --> 00:08:32,783
Erector sets, bicycles and Barbies.

238
00:08:32,963 --> 00:08:34,800
They circled what they wanted,

239
00:08:34,800 --> 00:08:36,025
dog-eared the pages,

240
00:08:36,025 --> 00:08:38,168
and wrote the item numbers on their

241
00:08:38,168 --> 00:08:39,209
letters to Santa.

242
00:08:39,389 --> 00:08:41,910
Brothers and sisters fought over who got

243
00:08:41,910 --> 00:08:42,478
it first.

244
00:08:42,828 --> 00:08:45,164
Parents used the Wish Book too,

245
00:08:45,164 --> 00:08:47,878
quietly, after the kids were in bed.

246
00:08:48,058 --> 00:08:50,332
They filled out the order form in

247
00:08:50,332 --> 00:08:52,330
pen, added the shipping cost,

248
00:08:52,330 --> 00:08:54,328
and mailed it in with a check

249
00:08:54,328 --> 00:08:55,500
or a money order.

250
00:08:55,680 --> 00:08:57,622
A couple of weeks later,

251
00:08:57,622 --> 00:09:01,345
a mysterious brown box arrived and disappeared

252
00:09:01,345 --> 00:09:04,097
into a closet until Christmas Eve.

253
00:09:04,447 --> 00:09:06,954
If you never ordered from a catalog,

254
00:09:06,954 --> 00:09:08,417
here's how it worked.

255
00:09:08,597 --> 00:09:10,291
You found what you wanted,

256
00:09:10,291 --> 00:09:12,051
wrote down the item number,

257
00:09:12,051 --> 00:09:13,876
the size and the color on an

258
00:09:13,876 --> 00:09:15,374
order form in the back,

259
00:09:15,374 --> 00:09:16,678
and added it all up.

260
00:09:16,858 --> 00:09:18,216
Then you mailed it,

261
00:09:18,216 --> 00:09:20,289
or later, called it in on the

262
00:09:20,289 --> 00:09:21,862
telephone, and waited.

263
00:09:22,212 --> 00:09:23,001
And waited.

264
00:09:23,181 --> 00:09:26,014
Delivery usually took one to three weeks.

265
00:09:26,194 --> 00:09:27,889
There was no tracking number.

266
00:09:28,069 --> 00:09:30,146
You just checked the mailbox,

267
00:09:30,146 --> 00:09:32,652
or listened for the delivery truck,

268
00:09:32,652 --> 00:09:34,944
or waited for the post office to

269
00:09:34,944 --> 00:09:35,302
call.

270
00:09:35,482 --> 00:09:37,495
When the package finally came,

271
00:09:37,495 --> 00:09:40,180
the whole family gathered around to open

272
00:09:40,180 --> 00:09:40,381
it.

273
00:09:40,732 --> 00:09:41,377
In a way,

274
00:09:41,377 --> 00:09:43,742
it was the online shopping of its

275
00:09:43,742 --> 00:09:44,029
day.

276
00:09:44,209 --> 00:09:45,495
A huge selection,

277
00:09:45,495 --> 00:09:47,234
delivered to your door,

278
00:09:47,234 --> 00:09:49,352
with a money-back guarantee.

279
00:09:49,532 --> 00:09:51,913
The only difference was that you used

280
00:09:51,913 --> 00:09:53,909
a pencil instead of a computer,

281
00:09:53,909 --> 00:09:56,161
and you needed a lot more patience.

282
00:09:56,512 --> 00:09:57,649
So what happened?

283
00:09:57,829 --> 00:09:59,900
In the seventies and eighties,

284
00:09:59,900 --> 00:10:01,556
the world changed again.

285
00:10:01,736 --> 00:10:04,599
New discount stores like Kmart and then

286
00:10:04,599 --> 00:10:06,728
Walmart offered lower prices.

287
00:10:06,908 --> 00:10:09,631
Specialty stores in the mall took away

288
00:10:09,631 --> 00:10:11,494
Sears' clothing customers.

289
00:10:11,674 --> 00:10:12,783
And the catalog,

290
00:10:12,783 --> 00:10:14,930
which had once been a lifeline,

291
00:10:14,930 --> 00:10:17,770
was becoming expensive to print and mail.

292
00:10:18,120 --> 00:10:20,096
In nineteen seventy-three,

293
00:10:20,096 --> 00:10:23,290
Sears finished the Sears Tower in Chicago,

294
00:10:23,290 --> 00:10:25,875
the tallest building in the world.

295
00:10:26,055 --> 00:10:28,040
It was a symbol of power.

296
00:10:28,220 --> 00:10:29,609
But by the eighties,

297
00:10:29,609 --> 00:10:31,692
the company was losing ground.

298
00:10:31,872 --> 00:10:34,905
It tried branching out into insurance with

299
00:10:34,905 --> 00:10:37,361
Allstate, stocks with Dean Witter,

300
00:10:37,361 --> 00:10:40,033
and real estate with Coldwell Banker.

301
00:10:40,213 --> 00:10:42,477
It even launched the Discover card.

302
00:10:42,827 --> 00:10:45,181
The catalog itself was losing money,

303
00:10:45,181 --> 00:10:48,188
reportedly more than a hundred million dollars

304
00:10:48,188 --> 00:10:50,084
a year by the early nineties.

305
00:10:50,264 --> 00:10:52,943
In January of nineteen ninety-three,

306
00:10:52,943 --> 00:10:56,218
Sears announced that the big general catalog

307
00:10:56,218 --> 00:10:56,963
would end.

308
00:10:57,143 --> 00:10:58,990
After ninety-seven years,

309
00:10:58,990 --> 00:11:01,281
the book that had sat in almost

310
00:11:01,281 --> 00:11:03,424
every American home was gone.

311
00:11:03,773 --> 00:11:05,109
The irony is painful.

312
00:11:05,289 --> 00:11:06,675
Just a year later,

313
00:11:06,675 --> 00:11:08,523
in nineteen ninety-four,

314
00:11:08,523 --> 00:11:10,986
a man named Jeff Bezos started a

315
00:11:10,986 --> 00:11:13,450
company that sold books by mail.

316
00:11:13,630 --> 00:11:15,349
It was called Amazon.

317
00:11:15,529 --> 00:11:16,535
In many ways,

318
00:11:16,535 --> 00:11:19,863
Amazon built exactly what the Sears catalog

319
00:11:19,863 --> 00:11:22,804
had been, a giant store that delivered

320
00:11:22,804 --> 00:11:25,049
almost anything to your door.

321
00:11:25,229 --> 00:11:27,865
Sears had the idea a century earlier,

322
00:11:27,865 --> 00:11:28,863
and let it go.

323
00:11:29,212 --> 00:11:30,982
In the years that followed,

324
00:11:30,982 --> 00:11:32,359
Sears kept shrinking.

325
00:11:32,539 --> 00:11:34,280
Store after store closed.

326
00:11:34,460 --> 00:11:36,412
In two thousand eighteen,

327
00:11:36,412 --> 00:11:38,988
the company filed for bankruptcy.

328
00:11:39,168 --> 00:11:41,957
Today, only a handful of Sears stores

329
00:11:41,957 --> 00:11:44,067
remain in the whole country,

330
00:11:44,067 --> 00:11:46,404
a shadow of the giant that once

331
00:11:46,404 --> 00:11:48,514
sold everything to everyone.

332
00:11:48,864 --> 00:11:51,153
But the memory of the catalog hasn't

333
00:11:51,153 --> 00:11:51,535
faded.

334
00:11:51,715 --> 00:11:54,257
Old copies sell to collectors today.

335
00:11:54,437 --> 00:11:56,761
People still talk about the toys they

336
00:11:56,761 --> 00:11:59,209
circled, the jeans that never wore out,

337
00:11:59,209 --> 00:12:01,658
and the tools their fathers bought that

338
00:12:01,658 --> 00:12:03,667
are still hanging in the garage.

339
00:12:03,847 --> 00:12:06,943
Craftsman tools came with a lifetime guarantee,

340
00:12:06,943 --> 00:12:09,314
and some of those wrenches are older

341
00:12:09,314 --> 00:12:10,500
than their owners.

342
00:12:10,849 --> 00:12:13,492
The Sears catalog was more than a

343
00:12:13,492 --> 00:12:13,973
store.

344
00:12:14,153 --> 00:12:16,171
It was a part of American family

345
00:12:16,171 --> 00:12:16,486
life.

346
00:12:16,666 --> 00:12:18,763
It connected farms to cities,

347
00:12:18,763 --> 00:12:20,208
dreams to doorsteps,

348
00:12:20,208 --> 00:12:22,088
and children to Christmas.

349
00:12:22,268 --> 00:12:23,712
If you grew up with it,

350
00:12:23,712 --> 00:12:25,972
you remember the smell of the paper,

351
00:12:25,972 --> 00:12:27,918
the weight of it in your hands,

352
00:12:27,918 --> 00:12:30,178
and that feeling of turning the page

353
00:12:30,178 --> 00:12:31,370
to the toy section.

354
00:12:31,720 --> 00:12:33,659
So tell us in the comments.

355
00:12:33,839 --> 00:12:35,634
What was the best thing you ever

356
00:12:35,634 --> 00:12:37,148
got from the Sears catalog?

357
00:12:37,328 --> 00:12:39,148
Did you circle toys in the Wish

358
00:12:39,148 --> 00:12:39,441
Book?

359
00:12:39,621 --> 00:12:41,629
Do you still have something from Sears

360
00:12:41,629 --> 00:12:42,686
in your house today?

361
00:12:42,866 --> 00:12:44,306
We read every comment.

362
00:12:44,486 --> 00:12:47,061
Next time, we're taking a walk down

363
00:12:47,061 --> 00:12:49,710
Main Street in nineteen fifty-seven.

364
00:12:49,890 --> 00:12:51,587
Thank you for spending this time with

365
00:12:51,587 --> 00:12:51,725
me.
